From b7facabb793ac56541cbfe6b71815c20325b656b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:34:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10203/11362] Untrack conductor bookkeeping state: it
 deadlocked every commit

Seven ops/.*_state.json escalation-dedupe ledgers were TRACKED while the
conductor rewrites them every few seconds. A test-suite mutation marker naming
them can therefore never go clean while the loop runs, so it refused every
hook-respecting commit indefinitely. The conductor itself was immune, because
it commits with --no-verify, so the entire cost fell on humans and agents --
five refused commits in one day, each needing a marker deleted and justified by
hand. A guard that has to be talked past five times a day is training people to
talk past it.

Safe to untrack, verified rather than assumed. Both load paths are built for
absence and return fresh state on OSError or JSONDecodeError, and
run_stop_authority.py documents its fail direction out loud: "Unreadable state
= fresh state: persistence restarts, which only DELAYS an action (fail toward
not killing)." That is the safe direction, and it was the specific protocol
concern I had about touching stop-authority. The cost on a fresh clone is that
deduplicated alerts re-fire once, which is self-correcting.

The files stay on disk; only the index entries go. Siblings ops/.agent_scopes/
and ops/.test_suite_mutation_runs/ were already ignored for exactly this
reason: per-run local state does not belong in the research record.

Recommended by an independent review that measured the deadlock and correctly
declined to fix it -- untracking touches the stop-authority and janitor
protocol, and an attribution carve-out would have weakened the gate instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
